WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.665
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.665 --> 00:00:02.040
STEPHANIE WONG:
So Trevor, I keep

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.623
hearing all about this machine
learning stuff, but a lot of it

00:00:04.623 --> 00:00:05.432
goes over my head.

00:00:05.432 --> 00:00:07.140
TREVOR HILLIS: I used
to think that, too.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.210
But it turns out, you can get
started with it pretty easily.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.980
In fact, I was able to
build a machine learning

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.300
model in just a few minutes.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:12.900
STEPHANIE WONG:
And all you needed

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.820
was a highly-trained
data scientist, right?

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:15.240
TREVOR HILLIS: Nope.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.410
Just some data and BigQuery.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:17.493
Here, let me show you how.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:25.950
Let's get started.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.660
The first step in any email
problem is gathering data.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.030
So I'm going to use publicly
available data on city bike

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.540
share usage and weather
in Austin, Texas.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.790
I'm going to try and predict
how the weather in Austin

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.777
affects the daily
trips per bike,

00:00:37.777 --> 00:00:40.110
which the city could use to
make decisions about pricing

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.568
of insurance for those bikes
or how many bikes to leave out

00:00:42.568 --> 00:00:43.830
on a rainy day.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.630
I started in the
BigQuery web console

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.360
and used the Create Model
command to name and train

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:48.870
my model.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.590
I specified that this is
a linear regression model

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:53.610
and that my input column,
or the value that I'm

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.950
going to be predicting,
is trips per bike.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:59.130
Next I use standard SQL to
choose the feature columns

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.110
or model inputs that
are going to be used

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:02.610
to predict trips per bike--

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.069
in this case, temperature
and precipitation data.

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:07.110
I then trained my model,
which will all happen

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.993
in just about 30 seconds.

00:01:08.993 --> 00:01:10.410
While the model
is training, let's

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.008
get our model ready
to be evaluated.

00:01:12.008 --> 00:01:13.800
This is how you'll find
out important stats

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.092
about your model, like how
useful it is for predictions

00:01:16.092 --> 00:01:17.573
and what your average error is.

00:01:17.573 --> 00:01:18.990
Once the model is
trained, you can

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.172
click the model in
your BQ data set, which

00:01:21.172 --> 00:01:23.130
will include information
on the training stats,

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.020
like the number of
iterations your model ran

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.100
and what your training loss
was and your learn rate.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.520
To evaluate our model, we
run the ML.EVALUATE command,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.000
referencing the name of the
model we want to evaluate,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.693
then selecting the
same feature columns

00:01:35.693 --> 00:01:37.610
we used to train our
model in our first query.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:40.700
Two columns to pay close
attention to are r-squared

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.220
score and mean squared error.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.648
You typically want an
r-squared score closer to 1.

00:01:45.648 --> 00:01:47.940
So there's a lot of room for
improvement in this model.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.550
There's a great article
in the description

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.133
that talks more about
how to interpret

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:52.190
the results for a model.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.930
But let's go ahead and use our
model to make a prediction.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.590
The PREDICT command
is almost identical

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.750
to our ML.EVALUATE
command, except we're

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.450
going to select a new column
for our predicted values

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:03.740
at the beginning of the query.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.200
And specify the ML.PREDICT
command, and voila,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.477
we have some predictions.

00:02:08.477 --> 00:02:11.060
STEPHANIE WONG: So there's still
a lot that I don't quite get.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.160
But I feel like I could
start using this today.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.680
TREVOR HILLIS: The details
can be pretty complicated.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.160
But this is a great way
to get started making

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.420
predictions and learning more.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:18.810
STEPHANIE WONG: Don't
forget to like, comment,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.290
and subscribe for more great
Google Cloud Platform content.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:24.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

